"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 6.9. The surgical findings included a pathological stage of pT2, no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymph nodes removed is a factor, as missing nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The biopsy demonstrated a Gleason 3+3 diagnosis, which aligns with the surgical findings.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of nodal involvement documented in the pathology report. The discrepancy between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is not explicitly detailed as a major driver, though the biopsy confirmed the Gleason grade. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are unknown, which significantly limits the certainty of recurrence risk assessment. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and subsequent trajectory would be crucial to revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 87.17 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 56.66–117.67 months; its coverage has not been validated."
